[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5476771664","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5476771664","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5476771664,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABRnDnUA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-08-31T10:02:12Z","updated_at":"2026-08-31T10:02:12Z","body":"Following up on the version point, because it is sharper than I put it above.\n\nMCP tools have no version. In the 2025-06-18 schema, `Tool extends BaseMetadata`, which is `name` plus an optional `title`, and adds `description`, `inputSchema`, an optional `outputSchema`, optional `annotations` and `_meta`. There is no version field on a tool anywhere in that schema. The only versioned thing is `Implementation`, which is the server.\n\nThe AgBOM `tool` component requires `version`. For an MCP-sourced tool that field cannot come from the tool. The natural filler is the server's version, which moves for reasons unrelated to any single tool's declaration, and does not move when a declaration is edited in place.\n\nTwo consequences for the gap above.\n\n`reason: component_upgraded` cannot fire from a tool's own declaration changing, because the declaration has nothing to bump. When it does fire it reports a server-level change and does not say which tool moved.\n\n`annotations` is where `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint` and `openWorldHint` live. It is optional, freely mutable at every `tools/list`, and it is the field with measured flips behind it. ACS has no surface that carries it and no event that fires when it changes.\n\nSo a declaration digest is not just the tidier option here. For MCP-sourced tools it is the only thing that can carry the signal, because the version field ACS leans on is not describing the tool in the first place.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5476771664/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5497760480","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5497760480","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5497760480,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR7Eq4A","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T17:23:46Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:23:46Z","body":"Ran your numbers against the deposit before writing, since the proposal leans on them: `binding_gap_pp: 24.46` is in `results.json` exactly, and `flips_v1.csv` carries exactly 17 rows matching `destructiveHint,true,false,permissive`, each with `decl_confirm_count` between 17 and 33. The figures hold as stated.\n\nOne word in the write-up deserves the bound the dataset itself declares. `results.json` carries `corroboration_caveat`: \"confirmations are repeated crawls by one crawler; independent-observer corroboration applies to the receipt network, not this baseline series\". So \"17 corroborated flips\" is stronger than the deposit claims for this series - \"confirmed (single-crawler, repeated)\" is the accurate strength. In a thread whose subject is precisely the distance between self-reported and independently observed, the word should carry its own caveat. The 17 flips lose nothing by it; they were confirmed 17 to 33 times each.\n\nOn the mechanism, one addition that I think your proposal needs to be enforceable rather than declarative.\n\n**The digest needs an observation channel, or it is the boolean one level up.** Item 2 carries `declaration_digest` on `steps/toolCallRequest` - computed by whom? If the harness computes it from the declaration it fetched, the Guardian ends up comparing a framework-computed value against a framework-registered value. That is the same trust shape the spec rejects for `digest_verified`, in the sentence you quote. For skills this is hard to avoid, since the Guardian may never hold the artifact. For MCP tools it is avoidable: `tools/list` is a client-initiated request/response, so wrapping it does not depend on the server volunteering anything - which was your (correct) objection to relying on `list_changed`. Today `extend_mcp.md` mentions `tools/call` twenty times and `tools/list` zero. Add a fifth item: wrap `tools/list`, and let the Guardian compute the digest from the declaration bytes it observed. Then the deny rule in item 4 binds to something the Guardian saw rather than something it was told - which would make the tool binding stronger than the skill precedent, not merely parallel to it.\n\n**The canonical form needs #18, and your references skip it.** Item 1 computes the digest \"over the canonical form\" of the declaration. On #36, ossumpossum stated the portability condition that applies here unchanged: a corpus \"is only portable if #18 pins a defined signing scope (exactly which bytes are inside each signature) and a canonical serialization... Otherwise two conformant verifiers can disagree\" on the same record. Two conformant implementations disagreeing on a declaration's digest means false mutations, and under item 4, spurious denies. If the digest definition references whatever canonicalization #18 lands, the two land as one decision instead of two half-decisions.\n\nThe #8 cross-reference raises the stakes in the same direction: if `security_context` lands, trust boundaries and `required_controls` become one more unversioned declaration surface - and a `required_controls` entry that silently disappears at unchanged version is the same measured class as the `destructiveHint` flips, a control switched off by the very field it reads. The digest scope in your item 1 (\"any protocol-level annotations\") would cover it as written; worth saying so there, since #8 is where that surface gets added.\n\nOne note on the cross-reference to OWASP/www-project-agentic-skills-top-10#63: the invocation-time re-evaluation proposed there (now Mitigation 7 in OWASP/www-project-agentic-skills-top-10#74) needs a recorded state to compare against, and your own thread there observes that for MCP tools nothing publishes a digest of record. A Guardian-computed `declaration_digest` over a wrapped `tools/list` is exactly that missing comparand - so the two proposals are not just the same gap at two layers, one is the mechanism the other's comparison step presupposes.\n\nOn your closing offer: the negative vector for this pairs naturally with the #30 suite shape - a same-version redefinition vector where the declaration mutates, `version` does not, and the expected verdict is deny-as-unverifiable. Happy to write it alongside yours whenever the direction is settled.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5497760480/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5498104236","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5498104236","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5498104236,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR7ZprA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:35Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:35Z","body":"All four land, and the first is a correction I should carry further than this thread.\n\nYou are right about \"corroborated\". results.json says it plainly: confirmations are repeated crawls by one crawler, and independent-observer corroboration applies to the receipt network rather than this baseline series. The accurate form is 17 confirmed permissive destructiveHint flips, true to false, each confirmed 17 to 33 times by repeated crawls from a single crawler. I have used the stronger word in three other places including my own FIXTURES.md, which does not carry the caveat at all. Fixing the body here and the repo. The point about a thread on declared-versus-observed being the wrong place to overstate a strength is well made.\n\nThe tools/list wrapping is better than what I proposed. A framework-computed digest compared against a framework-registered one is the digest_verified shape one level up, which is exactly what I quoted the spec rejecting. tools/list being client-initiated is what makes this avoidable for MCP where it is not avoidable for skills, and a Guardian computing the digest from bytes it observed makes the tool binding stronger than the skill precedent rather than parallel to it. extend_mcp.md mentioning tools/call twenty times and tools/list zero is the gap in one line. Adding it as item 5.\n\nOn #18, agreed, and I should have referenced it. A digest over an undefined canonical form is two conformant verifiers disagreeing about whether a declaration mutated, and under item 4 that is a spurious deny rather than a missed one. Item 1 will depend on whatever canonicalization #18 lands rather than restate one.\n\nOn #8, worth stating in item 1 explicitly: a required_controls entry that disappears at unchanged version is the same measured class as the destructiveHint flips, a control switched off by the field that reads it.\n\nYour reading of the AST07 relationship is sharper than mine. I had them as the same gap at two layers. One being the comparand the other's comparison step presupposes is the more useful statement, and it changes what Mitigation 7 can claim without it.\n\nOn the vector, yes, and it is yours to shape since #30 is your suite.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5498104236/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5499322914","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5499322914","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5499322914,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR8kCIg","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T19:33:09Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T19:33:09Z","body":"Taken, then. Shaped now, so it is concrete before it is code.\n\nFirst a measurement: none of the 19 vectors in the #30 branch touches the declaration surface - the suite judges an action against state, authorization and evidence, never a tool's own definition against what was vetted. So this is a new family, not a variant of an existing one. It lands as category 7, declaration integrity, in the suite's existing conventions: `state` carries what was bound at vetting time, `input` carries what the call presents, and the family ships with its positive control.\n\n- `neg-cat7-001` - observed declaration digest diverges from the bound one at unchanged `version`: REJECT, `DECLARATION_MISMATCH`, reason must mention the digest. The measured flip made executable - a declaration that moved with nothing for `agbom/changed` to carry.\n- `neg-cat7-002` - the deployment declares the binding but nothing was read about what the declaration currently says: UNMEASURABLE, `DECLARATION_UNOBSERVED`. The suite already refuses to convert absence of measurement into a verdict (cat 5's oracle rule); this is the same rule pointed at the declaration.\n- `pos-cat7-001` - observed digest equals the bound one at the same version: MUST pass. The check is on divergence, not on the field's presence.\n\nThe three are drafted against `vector.schema.json` and fail the current schema on exactly two points, both by construction: `category` is capped at 6, and the `code` enum is closed. Both are one-line edits when the family lands.\n\nThey stay out of the #30 branch until two things settle, deliberately: item 1's field shape landing in the spec, and #18's canonical form - a vector that encoded its own canonicalization would smuggle in exactly what item 1 now defers. Once those land, only input keys move if the field name changes.\n\nAnd M6 is the right way to carry a correction: recorded where the next reader works, not fixed in place.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5499322914/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5507044157","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5507044157","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5507044157,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSD7TPQ","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-02T08:55:54Z","updated_at":"2026-09-02T08:55:54Z","body":"Holding them until item 1's field shape and #18 land is the right call, and worth saying rather than just agreeing with: a vector carrying its own canonicalization would fix by example the thing item 1 deliberately defers, and that is very hard to unpick once it is in a suite people run.\n\nOne thing from the other side that may be worth carrying into the family.\n\nThe C9 suite hit the same shape and settled on three states in the record against two outcomes at the gate: BOUND, STALE and UNOBSERVED, where STALE means the contract moved under an unchanged declaration and UNOBSERVED means nothing attested either way. Both make the declaration unusable, so the gate does the same thing for both, but they stay separate in the record, because the coverage measure, declared minus verified, cannot be computed from a binary.\n\nYour category 7 has those same two shapes. neg-001 is we looked and it diverged. neg-002 is we never looked. In a deployment that fails closed both may resolve to the same action, and if they do, the verdicts converge and the records should not. A fleet carrying a hundred cat7-001s has a different problem from one carrying a hundred cat7-002s, and the second is the one that looks fine on a dashboard.\n\nSo the split you have drawn between REJECT and UNMEASURABLE is the part I would protect if anyone later argues they should collapse.\n\nOn M6, that is generous. It was written that way partly because the alternative was quietly changing a word in a file whose whole subject is records that stop describing what they record.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5507044157/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5509297191","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5509297191","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5509297191,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSGE0Jw","user":{"login":"dodogeny","id":174808042,"node_id":"U_kgDOCmtb6g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/174808042?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny","html_url":"https://github.com/dodogeny","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-02T12:10:10Z","updated_at":"2026-09-02T12:10:10Z","body":"The same-version redefinition case is the one that's hard to argue away, agbom/changed producing a diff whose before and after are byte-identical because the version field didn't move. And your point that drift-ranking exhausts its signal pool almost immediately, 5% of the population carrying any prior change, matches what I see. Re-auditing by \"what moved recently\" misses everything that's about to move for the first time.\n\nI work on Apitella (https://apitella.com/?utm_source=github&utm_medium=comment&utm_campaign=outreach-8), which does roughly the content-hash-over-canonical-declaration thing you describe in item 1: it snapshots MCP servers and reports digest changes at unchanged version, including destructiveHint and readOnlyHint flips specifically. Your 17 confirmed destructiveHint true-to-false flips line up with the kind of thing it surfaces. Would genuinely like to compare notes on the canonicalization piece, that's where it gets messy fast.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5509297191/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5543029802","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5543029802","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5543029802,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSmPsKg","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-04T15:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-09-04T15:53:38Z","body":"Taking the three-state record, and the reason you give for it is stronger than the one I had.\n\nI was holding REJECT and UNMEASURABLE apart on honesty grounds - nothing to compare is not nothing wrong, and a verdict should not be manufactured out of an absent measurement. Yours asks nobody to be scrupulous: declared minus verified is not computable from a binary, so collapsing the states destroys the measure whether or not anyone intended it. That argument holds in front of a team that does not share the discipline. Mine does not.\n\nChecked what the suite already does rather than assume it. `runner.py` compares the failure code unconditionally, on every vector including positive controls, not only where the verdict is REJECT - and that is not foresight, it came from astrogilda's review on #30 in August, which caught a positive control's code field going uncompared. So an adapter returning UNMEASURABLE with `DECLARATION_MISMATCH` where `DECLARATION_UNOBSERVED` is expected fails the vector today. The separation you would protect is enforced, by a path independent of the argument for it.\n\nWhere a vector's reach ends is worth stating, since it bears on your fleet point. The suite constrains the gate, not the record - it can require that a conformant implementation produce two distinct codes for the two shapes, and it cannot require that anything downstream keep them apart. But that is the useful half: a deployment that fails closed on both still has to emit different codes to pass, so the information the coverage measure needs exists at the boundary even where the action is identical. Anything past that is the consumer's problem, and no vector can reach it.\n\nYour argument is already doing work elsewhere on this repo. On #16 today I reviewed a reconciliation design whose output specifies coverage_status as an overall state plus per-capability states - and an overall state is the collapse you describe, one level up: the record has to stay per-rule, and the overall value has to be derived by a stated precedence or two implementations will disagree on the same findings. The comment is https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/16#issuecomment-5542586837, section 3, if the application is useful to you; the reasoning there is yours.\n\nOne observation from the other side, in case the shape is useful beyond declarations. I have hit the same collapse where nobody designed one: a test harness whose output showed a failure while its exit status stayed zero, and an aggregator that read only the exit status. The distinction was present in the record and absent from the thing counting - so the aggregate reported health that the underlying record contradicted. Different domain, same failure: the state that looks fine is the one that is not being read, rather than the one that is not being separated.\n\nAvp9.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5543029802/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5611228655","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5611228655","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5611228655,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABTnSN7w","user":{"login":"rocklambros","id":152409127,"node_id":"U_kgDOCRWUJw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/152409127?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros","html_url":"https://github.com/rocklambros","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-10T01:21:25Z","updated_at":"2026-09-10T01:21:25Z","body":"Triage note, ahead of the OWASP relaunch. This is now labelled `scope:deferred`, which means tracked and scheduled rather than rejected. The project has published a [Current Priority Scope](https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#current-priority-scope) committing the next ninety days to one outcome, a runnable Guardian reference implementation benchmarked against Microsoft AGT. Work that does not feed that outcome lands in the v0.2.0 window instead of being closed.\n\nThis one deserves more than a label. The Strategic Adoption Plan names it the strongest technical contribution the project has received from outside, and the gap is real: nothing in the `toolCallRequest` payload binds a call to the declaration the Guardian vetted, so a same-version redefinition is invisible. It is deferred only because closing it means adding an integrity binding to the wire, and the ninety-day window is committed to shipping a runnable Guardian against the v0.1.0 contract rather than changing that contract. Integrity binding is a strong candidate for the v0.2.0 agenda and this issue is where that discussion continues.\n\nNothing here needs re-filing. It stays open, keeps its labels, and moves to the backlog when the v0.2.0 window opens. If you think it belongs in the ninety-day window instead, say so on this thread and a maintainer will re-scope it.","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5611228655/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5618468428","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5618468428","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5618468428,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABTuMGTA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-10T12:11:19Z","updated_at":"2026-09-10T12:11:19Z","body":"Understood, and deferred is the right call. Shipping a Guardian against v0.1.0 and changing v0.1.0 inside the same ninety days are not compatible, and the runnable implementation is what the project needs first.\n\nNot asking for a re-scope. One thing for the record so it is here when the v0.2.0 window opens. The measurement this issue argues from has been published rather than deposited since I filed. Across 35 crawls of the public MCP registry between June and August, 44,172 tools on 2,043 servers: 83.8% declare a canonical effect annotation, 59.3% hold a declaration still bound to an unmutated contract. Same gap the issue body cites, now with both limbs stated rather than only the difference. doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22649163, with Gautam Bharti.\n\nHappy for it to sit until the window opens.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5618468428/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6314146333","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6314146333","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6314146333,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABeFo6HQ","user":{"login":"paolocostanzo","id":225715812,"node_id":"U_kgDODXQmZA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/225715812?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo","html_url":"https://github.com/paolocostanzo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-21T10:50:56Z","updated_at":"2026-10-21T10:50:56Z","body":"Adding the host side for the record, for when the v0.2.0 window opens. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nThe registry measurements above show declarations moving under an unchanged version. I measured what the host does when that happens: eight AI coding clients (six terminal clients with five runs per condition, each from a clean VM snapshot, plus two IDEs run by hand), October 2026. An approved MCP tool's description was changed mid-session, with and without `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n\n- None of the eight asked for approval again because of the change.\n- Without a notification, all eight stayed on the old declaration for the rest of the session.\n- With a notification, four fetched the new declaration, three of them without any notice to the user; three ignored the notification until restart; one never subscribes and could not receive it.\n- After a restart, every client observed used the new declaration without asking, except one whose approvals expire with the session.\n\nTwo things this adds to the proposal. On the host side, approval follows the tool name, not the declaration, so today nothing below the Guardian catches the change. And anything keyed on `list_changed` inherits the host's handling of it, which ranged from a silent refresh to ignoring it to not supporting it. Both point to item 5: compute the digest from an observed `tools/list` rather than wait for the notification.\n\nLimits: one OS in a VM, the client versions current on 15 to 17 October, only the description changed (not the schema or annotations), and the model's use of the new text was not observed.\n\nWrite-up: https://paolocostanzo.github.io/approved-mcp-tools-can-change-after-you-say-yes/\nLab and data: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23438372\n\nHappy to run the same harness against a Guardian on the MCP wrapping once item 5 has a shape.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6314146333/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6316773372","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6316773372","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6316773372,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABeIJP_A","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-21T13:04:53Z","updated_at":"2026-10-21T13:04:53Z","body":"This is the half the issue could not supply. The registry series measures declarations moving at unchanged version. Yours measures what happens downstream when one does, and the answer is that nothing re-asks. Neither half carries the argument alone.\n\nIt also strengthens item 5 past the reason I gave for it. I argued for wrapping `tools/list` because it is client-initiated and so does not depend on the server volunteering a notification. Your numbers make the harder case: even when the server does volunteer, four of eight act on it, three ignore it until restart, and one cannot receive it at all. So `list_changed` is unreliable on the host side as well as the server side, and a digest computed from an observed `tools/list` is the only shape that depends on neither.\n\nThe restart result is the one I did not expect. Every client took the new declaration after a restart without asking, and the single exception was the one whose approval expired with the session. Session-scoped approval is usually written up as a usability cost. On this evidence it is the only observed behaviour that prompts again, and approval persistence is what carries a stale binding across the boundary.\n\nWhere the two series do not yet meet, and I mean this as the next condition rather than a gap in yours. You changed the description. The registry series' sharpest result is the 17 confirmed permissive `destructiveHint` flips, which is an annotation change, and your write-up notes the server was user-configured in a pre-trusted folder so repository-declared servers are out of scope. Item 1's digest covers name, description, parameter schema and protocol-level annotations as one canonical form, so both conditions sit inside its scope and neither is measured yet.\n\nOn your offer, the states are already designed in this thread rather than waiting to be invented. @avp9-nexus's category 7 carries `neg-cat7-001` for an observed digest diverging from the bound one and `neg-cat7-002` for nothing having been read, against a three-state record of BOUND, STALE and UNOBSERVED. Your eight clients already distribute across those three. Both are held until item 1's field shape and #18's canonicalization land, which is the right order.\n\nStill not asking for a re-scope.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6316773372/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":1,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465192706","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6465192706","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6465192706,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABgVsDAg","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-28T09:17:50Z","updated_at":"2026-10-28T09:17:50Z","body":"A measurement for the v0.2.0 record. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nWe maintain HISTOR, an open record of MCP tool definitions. It periodically attempts tools/list on registry-listed remote endpoints and records the lists it successfully observes. We checked how often those lists change without a corresponding version signal.\n\nThe 20 October snapshot contains 21,543 remote-server tool-list changes observed between 24 September and 20 October. Using version pairs recovered from the log’s retained observations, serverInfo.version was identical before and after in 17,401 of the 21,401 changes with both values available (81.3%). The comparison uses the last run that served the old list and the run that observed the new one. These are server-reported version strings, not independently verified deployment identities.\n\nSeparately, we joined the snapshot to a registry export collected on 28 October. Of 21,351 changes that could be assessed against that export, 17,409 (81.5%, across 6,929 server endpoints) had no registry publication timestamp between the previous recorded change—or first pin—and the current change. Of the 15,936 such events with field-level comparisons, 4,831 changed only tool descriptions among the fields retained by the snapshot: no tools were added or removed, and neither input nor output schemas changed.\n\nThe runtime-version equality test and the registry no-publication test both held for 15,351 of the 21,209 changes where both could be assessed (72.4%). These tests use different observation intervals; this is their intersection, not a direct comparison of two historically captured registry-version values.\n\nMethod and limits:\n- The registry test counts any publication under the same server name within its interval, whether related to the observed change or not. Extending that interval seven days before its starting point, to allow for delayed rollouts, leaves 10,584 events (49.6%).\n- The two largest registry namespaces account for about a third of the 17,409 events. Excluding both leaves 11,585 of 14,777 assessed changes (78.4%).\n- The registry export excludes deleted entries. These publication-history figures describe the records available in that export; they do not establish complete historical release coverage or which version was actually deployed.\n- This analysis excludes packages. Annotations are also outside the October 20 comparison: HISTOR began retaining them separately on October 22.\n- Polling can miss intermediate states. Counts refer to observed tool-list transitions, not unique releases, attacks, or individual tool edits.\nFor the proposal: MCP defines no per-tool version field; an ACS integration must supply one. If it uses a registry or server-reported version, the measurements show why version equality alone cannot establish declaration continuity.\n\nAdded and removed tools are already representable in agbom/changed. The narrower gap is an in-place description or schema edit: if the other registered fields remain unchanged, a tool component populated only with the standard fields does not expose that edit. A digest binding computed from an observed declaration would provide the comparison, provided its defined scope preserves the changed fields.\n\nOn #18: the snapshot retains normalized tool records, not just digests. toolsets.jsonl.gz preserves name, description, inputSchema, and outputSchema when present. Of the snapshot’s 23,821 changes, 21,902 have field-level comparisons; the other 1,919 lack a digestible tool list on at least one side and have no field-level declaration comparison.\n\nThose retained fields can be re-canonicalized, but the snapshot cannot reconstruct omitted annotations or an arbitrary future full-declaration digest. It can support canonicalization experiments within its preserved scope.\n\nData, CC BY 4.0: [20 October snapshot](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512760). The runtime-version pairs are available separately through the [changes API](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/changes), backfilled from retained observations where available. Historical registry-version fields remain null; the registry figures above use the separate publication-history export.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465192706/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465748971","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6465748971","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6465748971,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABgWN_6w","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-28T09:46:56Z","updated_at":"2026-10-28T09:46:56Z","body":"A measurement I did not have, and it lands on the one thing the registry series could not reach.\n\nThe registry series measured the size of the binding gap, 24.46 percentage points across 44,172 tools. Paolo's added what happens downstream when a declaration moves. Yours measures how often it moves without a version signal at all: 17,401 of 21,401 changes with both values available carried an identical serverInfo.version, 81.3 percent, across a month of observed transitions. That is the issue title, measured, at a scale the 35 crawl observations behind it cannot speak to. The registry no-publication test arriving at 81.5 percent by a different route, and the 72.4 percent intersection, matter more to me than either number alone, and I take the point that those are different observation intervals rather than two captured registry versions.\n\nOne boundary, and then a correction to my own issue body. You note annotations sit outside the 20 October comparison because HISTOR only began retaining them on 22 October, so your 81.3 percent speaks to the description and schema class rather than the annotation class. Item 1 scopes the digest over name, description, parameter schema and protocol-level annotations, so three of those four now have population-scale evidence from your snapshot and the fourth does not.\n\nThe annotation class is not unmeasured, though, and I cited the weaker thing for it above. The 17 permissive destructiveHint flips are a subset. The binding paper carries 783 confirmed declaration changes, and the finding that bears on item 1 is that all four annotation defaults are restrictive, so a first declaration on the permissive pole takes a tool from assumed-restricted to declared-permissive with no change event to fire on at all. 86.0 percent of first declarations land permissive, holding between 83.4 and 94.0 percent under the omission of any single publisher. Under the schema conditional a re-approval trigger would have to fire on 445 tools where a watcher keyed to value flips reaches 62. That is a stronger argument for keeping annotations inside the digest scope than drift alone is, because a digest changes on a first declaration and a flip-watcher cannot fire on one. Paper, CC BY 4.0, Bharti and Agnihotri: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22649164\n\nThe figure I keep coming back to is a different one. 21,902 of your 23,821 changes have field-level comparisons, so 1,919 do not, lacking a digestible tool list on at least one side. That is 8.1 percent. On 4 September avp9-nexus argued for a three-state record that holds REJECT and UNMEASURABLE apart, on the grounds that a verifier should not report a failure it cannot distinguish from an absence. UNMEASURABLE now has a frequency, measured by someone who was not in that exchange. An 8.1 percent unmeasurable rate is an argument for the third state being normative rather than advisory.\n\nOn #18, I will take the corpus offer with the scope you gave it. Name, description, inputSchema and outputSchema re-canonicalized is item 1's scope minus annotations, which is enough to test whether a canonical form is stable across real declarations and not enough to test the annotation path. That is worth knowing before a field shape is fixed rather than after, and it is the half of the vector work in #29 and #30 that can proceed without waiting on the field definition.\n\nNot asking for a re-scope either. v0.2.0 is milestoned to March, and a record built from four independent sources over five months is a better thing to open that window with than a faster decision would have been.\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465748971/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545176155","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6545176155","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6545176155,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABhh92Ww","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-31T15:09:28Z","updated_at":"2026-10-31T15:09:28Z","body":"@Mayur021, on the 1,919: I looked at what they are, for the v0.2.0 record. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nI worked from the 20 October snapshot (HISTOR, https://histor.modelmarket.dev, snapshot 2026-10-20, CC BY 4.0, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512760; its `changes.jsonl.gz` and `servers.jsonl.gz` match the SHA-256 values in its `manifest.json`) and from HISTOR's public code at the last commit before the export, [`410ee41`](https://github.com/alexar76/histor/tree/410ee418a7116639cb2a3e477599108e1acb8588).\n\n- All 1,919 have `\"detail\": \"names-only\"` in their summary, which HISTOR writes when one side of a change has no tool-set digest (`histor/diffing.py`, line 171). As alexar76 wrote, HISTOR records the lists it successfully observes: an observation that fails, a 401 for instance, produces no change record, so none of the 1,919 is a declaration that went unread.\n- `histor/subject.py` leaves a set without a digest for one of two reasons. Either the set is malformed (an empty set, a duplicate name, or a malformed entry: `MTL-SUBJ-002`, `003` and `001`), or a schema in it holds a number that MTL/1, the label profile HISTOR digests under, declines: a fraction, an exponent, or an integer outside the interoperable range (`MTL-NUM-001`).\n- In 1,890 of the 1,919 the newer side has no digest (in 1,374 neither side has one), and its signed continuity label carries the newer side's reason. I drew 250 of them, stratified in proportion between the fleet below and the rest, and read the labels from HISTOR's API: all 250 say `MTL-NUM-001` and none says `MTL-SUBJ`, which puts malformed newer sets below about 3 percent of the 1,890 at 95 percent confidence. In the other 29 only the older side lacks a digest, and the code on their label is a default, so I left them out.\n- One publisher's fleet accounts for 794 of the 1,919. Without it the rate is 5.75 percent (1,125 of 19,564).\n\nSo the 8.1 percent does not count declarations that went unread. It is, almost entirely, declarations that arrived and that a canonical form declined to digest because of a number.\n\nMTL/1 digests the RFC 8785 form of the tool set, but refuses those numbers on the ground that two implementations may serialise them differently (`histor/subject.py`, lines 100-103). Plain RFC 8785 writes a number from its IEEE 754 double value, so it would digest a fraction, and a large integer may not survive it exactly. That is the other side of the #18 point from 1 September: a form that serialises these numbers risks two verifiers disagreeing, which item 4 turns into spurious denies, while a form that withholds the digest sends them to the third state. On this snapshot, with one digest over the whole tool set, that choice is behind about one recorded change in twelve, so whichever canonical form #18 lands has to make it explicitly, and item 1 has to say what the binding records when no digest can be computed. Item 1 digests per tool rather than per set, so its own exposure would be smaller, and I have not measured it. It also bears on the corpus test: `toolsets.jsonl.gz` is keyed by digest (the snapshot's README), and HISTOR writes a set's content only when it has one (`histor/crawler.py`, lines 906 and 1048), so by construction the corpus holds none of these numbers, and a canonical-form test on it will not meet them.\n\nFor the three-state record, these fit none of the three as drawn: the declaration was read, so they are not UNOBSERVED, and with no digest they are neither BOUND nor STALE. At the gate they would sit under UNMEASURABLE next to `neg-cat7-002`, for a reason the category 7 vectors do not name yet: `neg-cat7-002` is the case where nothing was read, and here a declaration was read and could not be reduced to a digest. HISTOR's labels keep the two apart under one verdict: both end in an `inconclusive` continuity label, a failed observation's with its status and no change record, an undigestible set's with its reason code. Its server filter does not: `state=unobserved` selects servers without a tool-set digest (`histor/store.py`), so it holds both. If the third state becomes normative, I would have it carry that reason, so the two do not collapse into one cell; how often it applies is set by #18 as much as by the declarations. I can add a vector for the second case once item 1's field shape and #18's canonical form land, since whether such a number has a digest at all is #18's decision.\n\nLimits: the code is the public mirror at that commit, which may differ from what ran; `labels.py` and `diffing.py` are unchanged there since 23 September, but `crawler.py` changed thirteen times between 10 and 18 October, inside the window, so for earlier changes I am assuming the same labelling. The reason codes come from a sample of 250, not a census, and I did not open `toolsets.jsonl.gz` or verify the labels' signatures. @alexar76, if I have misread how a change gets its label, please say so.\n\nAvp9.\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545176155/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545318826","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6545318826","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6545318826,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABhiGjqg","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-31T15:21:15Z","updated_at":"2026-10-31T15:21:15Z","body":"Two results for the canonicalization test, one of which covers the annotation path the 26 October corpus could not.\n\n**Annotations included, across two independent readers.** A second public record (heldfast) digests name, title, description, inputSchema, outputSchema, annotations and icons per tool, RFC 8785. Its checkpoint and our snapshot are from the same day, 26 October, about a minute apart. Recomputing its digest from our stored definitions: 23,190 servers in both records; on the 21,215 where we hold annotations, 315,605 of 318,389 tools (99.1%) produce the same digest byte for byte. Our implementation of the canonical form agrees with rfc8785 on 200,000 random doubles, with the Vercel AI SDK's JavaScript fingerprint on 6,882 stored tools, and with heldfast's and AgentAvow's published vectors. The canonical form held on real declarations. The two records still disagreed on 2,784 tools, and that is what bears on the field shape.\n\n**Most disagreement is capture, not content.** Of the 444 servers that differ, 148 were read at different moments around an edit or a failed read, and 218 ran different package versions. 78 differ only in how they were read: network or none at launch (24), the reader's own home directory or placeholder credential echoed into a description (24), outputSchema withheld from a client without the protocol-version header (15), only the first `tools/list` page read (10), other launch differences (5). A digest cannot distinguish these from a redefinition. That is a third shape beside STALE and UNOBSERVED: both sides looked, they disagree, and the disagreement says nothing about the server. If item 1 fixes a digest, it may need a capture record beside it (negotiated protocol version, whether every page was read, launch conditions), so a verifier can return UNMEASURABLE for that case instead of STALE. A draft of such a block: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/interop-digests-2026-10-31/capture-block.md\n\n**What a narrower field set misses.** Over our month of changes, 6.9% of modified tool definitions changed only outputSchema or only the top-level additionalProperties, which a description-schema-title fingerprint does not see. Annotation-only changes on 101 of the 2,784 differing tools above moved the digest only because annotations were in it.\n\nData, scripts and pinned inputs: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/interop-digests-2026-10-31/.\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545318826/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545577131","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6545577131","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6545577131,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABhiWUqw","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-31T15:44:40Z","updated_at":"2026-10-31T15:44:40Z","body":"@avp9-nexus, you read it right, including the parts you marked as limits.\n\n- The code that decides these labels did not change inside the window. In the source we deploy from, `tool_set_digest` in `subject.py`, the `names-only` summary in `diffing.py` and the continuity label in `labels.py` are as they were on 23 September. The two 10 October commits to `subject.py` add the package name to the descriptor and a namespace constant.\n- Leaving out the 29 was right. When only the older side lacks a digest, the label takes the newer set's hazard, a digestible set has none, and the code falls back to `MTL-NUM-001` (`labels.py`, line 236) whatever the older side's reason was. That code is a default, not a finding, and the label shouldn't state it.\n- The `state=unobserved` filter does hold both cases. The labels keep them apart, and the filter should too.\n\nYour corpus point also applies to my comment above. The 23,190 servers there are the ones where HISTOR holds a tool-set digest. Another 3,324 that both records read with tools on 26 October (12.5%, 1,867 of them from one publisher) have a HISTOR reading and no digest, so they were left out, and HISTOR keeps no copy of a set it cannot digest. \"The canonical form held on real declarations\" holds for the declarations MTL/1 digests; the numbers you describe are the ones it did not meet. If HISTOR kept those sets, heldfast's fingerprints on the same servers would test #18's question directly: whether two implementations serialise such numbers the same way.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6545577131/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572123939","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6572123939","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6572123939,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABh7qnIw","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-02T08:03:10Z","updated_at":"2026-11-02T08:03:10Z","body":"@avp9-nexus I pulled HISTOR at 410ee41 and checked your reading against the code rather than taking it. It holds. diffing.py writes names-only exactly where you say, the MTL-SUBJ codes map to your list in order, and crawler.py 907 and 1049 write a toolset blob only when a digest exists, which is the by-construction point.\n\nThe step that could have broken it does not. labels.py line 236 falls back to a hardcoded MTL-NUM-001, and fallback_subject returns hazard=None for a malformed set, so on its face a SUBJ case could be labelled NUM-001 and your 250-sample bound would be guaranteed rather than measured. It survives because crawler.py 1027 passes no_digest_code explicitly on the subject_error path, so the hardcoded default can only fire where the newer side has a digest and the older does not. That is the 29 you excluded, and excluding them was right.\n\nYour corpus point also lands on what I took on from alexar76 on 28 October. If the toolset store holds only sets that had a digest, a canonicalization test on it cannot meet the numbers that have none, so that class has to be synthesized. The per-tool exposure is worse: for a NUM-001 subject the descriptor is stored and the entries are not, so nobody can recompute it from the public record at any granularity.\n\nOn the two of you together: these are two different reasons for one state, and they land on different items. Your class has no digest, so item 4 never fires and item 1 has no field for it. alexar76's class has two digests that differ for a reason that is not the server, so item 4 does fire. As written it denies the 73 capture-explained servers, which is a defect in my item rather than in his measurement. Worth noting his results.json labels the remaining 5 unexplained rather than capture, so it is 73 and a residual.\n\nWhat I now think item 1 has wrong. Its {algorithm, value} shape is copied from skill_fields.definition.digest. That shape is right wherever the thing hashed is a complete artifact: CISA's 2026 SBOM minimum elements added Component Hash Value and Component Hash Algorithm in July, and the G7 SBOM for AI added Model hash value and Model hash algorithm in May, both over \"an executable component artifact\", both pointing at the IANA registry. A tool declaration is the exception, because its scope is a selection of fields rather than an artifact, and the interop work above found five public formats choosing five different field sets and four different conventions for an absent member, all already on RFC 8785. So whatever canonical form #18 lands cannot make two conformant verifiers agree, and item 1 needs a profile identifier beside algorithm and value that fixes both the field set and the absent-member rule, with item 4's deny conditioned on both sides declaring the same profile.\n\nFor the no-digest field there is a precedent worth copying rather than inventing: both of those documents say that where the author cannot reach the artifact, the value is recorded as unknown.\n\n@alexar76 one question, because it decides whether the four-member scope is complete. In the 6.9 percent, which path is \"the top-level additionalProperties\"? If it is inside inputSchema it is invisible only to the Vercel fingerprint, since @ai-sdk/mcp forces that member. If it sits beside inputSchema on the tool object then a four-member scope drops it too, and the 1,270 are invisible to MTL/1 and to the scope I took.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572123939/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572484190","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6572484190","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6572484190,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABh8AmXg","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-02T08:23:32Z","updated_at":"2026-11-02T08:23:32Z","body":"@Mayur021 Inside `inputSchema`: it is the root-level `additionalProperties` of the input schema. HISTOR records each change per tool member, with paths from that member's root, and the 1,270 are the modified tool entries whose only changed path is `$.additionalProperties` under `inputSchema`. A member beside `inputSchema` on the tool object could not be among them: an MTL/1 entry carries only name, description, inputSchema and outputSchema, so such a change moves no digest and is never recorded. So the 1,270 are visible to MTL/1 and to any scope that keeps `inputSchema` whole, and invisible only to a fingerprint that rewrites that member, like the Vercel one. The rest of the 6.9% is the 11,724 entries that changed only `outputSchema`, which any scope including `outputSchema` sees.\n\n\"Top-level additionalProperties\" in my comment was ambiguous; the dataset README says \"of the input schema\". The counts reproduce from the public 26 October snapshot (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/2026-10-26/changes.jsonl.gz, `summaryJson.modified[].fields`): 188,947 modified tool entries, 11,724 outputSchema-only, 1,270 inputSchema-only at `$.\"additionalProperties\"`, 6.88%.\n\nYou are also right about the 78. results.json has 73 explained by how each side read the server and 5 unexplained (same npm version, offline on both sides); my comment put those 5 under \"other launch differences\", and it should not have.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572484190/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572965082","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6572965082","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6572965082,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABh8d82g","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-02T08:51:34Z","updated_at":"2026-11-02T08:51:34Z","body":"That settles it, thank you. If the 1,270 sit at $.additionalProperties inside inputSchema, then any scope that keeps inputSchema whole sees them, and the only thing the 6.88 percent leaves open is outputSchema.\n\nWhich lands on item 1 again. Its scope as filed reads \"name, description, parameter schema, and any protocol-level annotations\" and never names outputSchema, so as written it misses your 11,724 outputSchema-only entries, 6.2 percent of modified tool entries on that snapshot. That is the second correction item 1 needs, and it is mine rather than a question for #18: the scope has to name outputSchema explicitly instead of leaving it inside \"parameter schema\".\n\nYour phrase \"keeps inputSchema whole\" is the part I had not seen. A profile identifier that fixes only the field set and the absent-member convention is still not enough, because two verifiers can agree on both and disagree anyway because one normalises a member before hashing. The Vercel case is exactly that: inputSchema is in scope and additionalProperties is rewritten inside it. So the profile has to fix three things, the field set, the absent-member rule, and whether any member in scope is transformed before the digest is computed.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6572965082/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6573119669","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6573119669","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6573119669,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABh8nYtQ","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-02T09:00:22Z","updated_at":"2026-11-02T09:00:22Z","body":"For a worked example of a profile that fixes all three: section 5.2 of the MTL/1 profile, https://github.com/alexar76/aicom/blob/main/awr/adoption/mcp-trust-label/PROFILE.md#52-normalisation\n\n- Field set: exactly name, description, inputSchema and outputSchema; title, annotations, _meta and anything else are dropped.\n- Absent members: a missing or non-string description becomes \"\", a missing or non-object inputSchema becomes {}, and outputSchema is present only if the server sent an object there, because absent and {} are different output contracts.\n- Transformation: none inside a member. The schemas are digested whole, and only the serialisation is fixed (RFC 8785).\n\nItem 1 could name such a profile by identifier rather than restate the rules, which also gives the verifier something to compare before it compares digests.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6573119669/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6573529015","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6573529015","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6573529015,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABh9AXtw","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-02T09:22:05Z","updated_at":"2026-11-02T09:22:05Z","body":"That is the mechanism, and your last line is the part I had underweighted. Naming the profile gives a verifier something to compare before it compares digests, so a profile mismatch is diagnosable without computing anything. That is what makes conditioning item 4's deny on a shared profile work in practice rather than only in principle.\n\nYour 5.2 also already answers the versioning half. The note that a future MTL version may digest more fields, \"which is why mtl is inside the descriptor and the version is inside every method id\", is the placement item 1 should copy. A profile identifier is not an addition to the design, it is where the design already puts the thing that changes every digest.\n\nOne reason item 1 cannot simply name MTL/1 as its profile. 5.2 drops annotations, and annotations are the class this issue was filed about: the measurement in the body is 17 permissive destructiveHint flips. Your pattern-set section says the price plainly, that under MTL/1 those members \"are not scanned and a label says nothing about them\", which is right for a claim bound to that digest. So an ACS profile either keeps annotations in the field set, or item 1 carries two digests the way HISTOR keeps title, annotations, icons and _meta beside the tool-set digest and digests them separately. I think the second is the better shape, because it leaves the four-member digest directly comparable with yours.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6573529015/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6631766825","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6631766825","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6631766825,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABi0i7KQ","user":{"login":"MK023","id":49072576,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ5MDcyNTc2","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/49072576?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023","html_url":"https://github.com/MK023","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MK023/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-04T13:23:03Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T13:23:03Z","body":"Adding the annotation condition for the record. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\n@Mayur021 named it on 21 October as the next condition ([comment](https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6316773372)): @paolocostanzo's host measurements changed only the tool description, and the registry's sharpest result is an annotation change. The scenario is Paolo's, the \"tool-definition canary\" from the OWASP draft \"AI Red Teaming Containment Best Practices\" (in review in #team-genai-redteam). He reviewed the protocol before anything ran, and this run adds the Guardian side.\n\n**Setup.** Claude Code 2.1.316 (`claude-opus-5-5`, MCP `2026-07-28`) in front of the reference Guardian, `integration` at `3183ecf`. The MCP server is a modified copy of Paolo's `q3-server.mjs` (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23438372) with one tool, `q3_noop`. The change keeps name, description and schema, and moves only the annotations: A has `readOnlyHint: true`, B has `readOnlyHint: false, destructiveHint: true`. Paolo's three phases: a call with A, the change, the same call in the same session, then a new session. Three cells: no change, a change with no signal, and a change with `list_changed`. An automatic arm (`-p`, 3 runs per cell) and a manual one (one session per cell), both in `default` permission mode.\n\n**Host.**\n- No signal: the session does not re-read the list, and the next call reaches the server in state B (4 of 4 sessions in that cell: 3 automatic runs, 1 manual).\n- `list_changed`: the host re-reads the list 3 to 15 ms after the notification (4 of 4 sessions: 15, 9 and 8 ms automatic, 3 ms manual).\n- No warning to the user in any cell, and the call goes through.\n\n**Approval.** With a Guardian in front, the host never asks. The deployment hooks `PreToolUse` on `mcp__.*`, and when the Guardian allows, the shim answers `permissionDecision: \"allow\"`, which the Claude Code docs say skips the permission prompt. The transcripts record all 36 calls of the two arms (27 automatic, 9 manual) as accepted with `source: \"hook\"`. So the host's own approval is replaced by the Guardian's decision.\n\nIn a separate run outside the frozen protocol, with the shipped `settings.json` (hook on `Bash` and `WebFetch` only), the prompt appeared on the first call. \"Don't ask again\" saved a rule with the bare tool name, `mcp__q3-lab__q3_noop`, and after the change every call was accepted by that rule without a prompt. That includes the `list_changed` cell, where the host had already re-read B. Paolo's [\"approval follows the tool name, not the declaration\"](https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6314146333) was also observed for annotations, in default permission mode, without the hook on MCP tools.\n\n**Guardian.** All 36 `steps/toolCallRequest` envelopes, before and after the change, are identical once ids and timestamps are removed: `tool: {name: \"mcp__q3-lab__q3_noop\"}`, the arguments and `raw_command`. All 36 were allowed. With a Guardian in front, the decision that replaces the host's approval is taken on an envelope that cannot show the change. That is this issue's gap, measured at the point where it decides the call.\n\n**Model.** The annotations never reach it. That doesn't contradict the MCP spec: annotations are hints, and clients MUST consider them untrusted unless they come from trusted servers ([Tools, 2026-07-28](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools)). In all 24 model requests that carry the tool across the two arms (plus the 6 of the separate run), the definition is the same before and after: `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, `eager_input_streaming`, `defer_loading`, and no annotations. For an annotation-only change, the host fetched B in the `list_changed` cell, but nothing it showed the user, sent the model or sent the Guardian carried the change.\n\n**Method.** The protocol was frozen with SHA-256 sums before any run and reviewed by Paolo. Each arm was sealed before the next one was opened. The raw data are kept: server logs, Guardian envelope extracts and the tool definitions sent to the model, CC BY 4.0. I can share them on request.\n\n**Limits.** One host, one version, one model. Three automatic runs and one manual session per cell. The no-hook run is a single manual session per cell, declared outside the protocol. Annotations only, not first declarations, and one direction only: from `readOnlyHint: true` to `readOnlyHint: false, destructiveHint: true`, not the permissive direction of the registry flips (`destructiveHint` from true to false).","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6631766825/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6688178696","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6688178696","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6688178696,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABjqWCCA","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-06T15:13:59Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T15:13:59Z","body":"@Mayur021, on the no-digest field, for the v0.2.0 record. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nThe precedent you cite records unknown where the author cannot reach the artifact. The class from 31 October is the other case: the declaration was read, and the canonical form declined to digest it (on that snapshot, almost always a number MTL/1 refuses, `MTL-NUM-001`). If unknown ends up covering both, a record cannot tell a declaration nobody read from one that was read and could not be reduced to a digest. That is the collapse this thread argued against in early September: the two may get the same action at the gate, and the record still has to keep them apart.\n\nA shape that keeps them apart without inventing much: unknown plus a reason from a closed list, at least `not_observed` (nothing was read) and `not_digestible` (read, and declined by the profile), with the profile's own code where it gives one. With a profile identifier beside it, `not_digestible` is stated relative to that profile, which is accurate: another profile might digest the same declaration.\n\nFor category 7 that means two UNMEASURABLE vectors rather than one. `neg-cat7-002` stays the `not_observed` case (`DECLARATION_UNOBSERVED`), and the case from 31 October becomes its own UNMEASURABLE vector with a code of its own. Both still wait for item 1's field shape and #18's canonical form.\n\nAvp9.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6688178696/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6714773325","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6714773325","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6714773325,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABkDtPTQ","user":{"login":"kenneives","id":101359438,"node_id":"U_kgDOBgqfTg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/101359438?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives","html_url":"https://github.com/kenneives","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenneives/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-08T00:00:57Z","updated_at":"2026-11-08T00:00:57Z","body":"\nFor the v0.2.0 record, not asking for a re-scope. Item 1 now needs a profile identifier, so here is another published profile with its choices on the three points @Mayur021 listed, plus numbers.\n\n`agentavow.mcp-tool-definition.v1`, also published in TRACE as `trace.mcp-tool-definition.v1` (same rules, different label):\n\n- Field set: name, title, description, inputSchema, outputSchema, annotations. `_meta` and unknown members are dropped. Annotations stay inside the one per-tool digest, so this is the single-digest shape rather than the two-digest one. An annotation-only change moves it; a separate packet tests `readOnlyHint` true to false and nothing else.\n- Absent members: a missing or null member is omitted. No default is filled in, so a missing description and `\"\"` give different digests. MTL/1 makes the other choice.\n- Transformation: none inside a member. Only the serialisation is fixed (RFC 8785).\n- Numbers: every number is read as an IEEE 754 double, as `JSON.parse` does, so a fraction gets a digest and an integer beyond 2^53 is hashed at its double value. A reader that keeps the exact integer computes a different digest. That is the opposite of `MTL-NUM-001`, and our vectors do not exercise it yet.\n- Label: the profile string is inside the preimage, `JCS({\"profile\": ..., \"tool\": ...})`, so digests under two profiles cannot be equal, and a verifier can compare labels before digests.\n- Not digestible: a definition JCS cannot represent at all (NaN, Infinity) gets a digest under a distinct label that never equals a portable one. That is close to @avp9-nexus's `not_digestible`, recorded as a value rather than as unknown.\n\nThe digest is per tool, keyed `tool:<name>`, with an encoding for non-ASCII and overlong names (13 vectors).\n\nOutside reproduction: @alexar76 recomputed the three signed DeepWiki digests from HISTOR's own 26 October read and got the same values (AgentAvow #240).\n\nFor comparison: the AISVS 1.02-dev glossary entry for Tool Definition (merged 4 November in OWASP/AISVS#1204) lists the same six members and excludes `_meta`.\n\nVectors (Apache-2.0): https://github.com/AgentAvow/AgentAvow/tree/main/docs/standards/tool-manifest-digest-vectors-v1\nAnnotation-only packet: https://github.com/AgentAvow/AgentAvow/tree/main/docs/standards/tool-manifest-digest-vectors-v1-annotations\n\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6714773325/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6718278820","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6718278820","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6718278820,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABkHDMpA","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-11-08T06:33:40Z","updated_at":"2026-11-08T06:33:40Z","body":"@avp9-nexus @kenneives, two facts about MTL/1 for the v0.2.0 record, since both of your comments describe it as it was before 31 October. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\n**Numbers.** @kenneives, the number rule you give for `agentavow.mcp-tool-definition.v1` has been MTL/1's rule since revision 1.1 (31 October): every number is read as its IEEE 754 double and written the RFC 8785 way, so a fraction gets a digest and an integer beyond 2^53 is hashed at its double value. A parser that keeps such an integer exact must convert it first (§5.4). `MTL-NUM-001` now covers only a number that has no double, such as `1e400`: your profile digests that under a separate label, MTL/1 gives it no digest and records why. Your point on absent members stands: MTL/1 writes a missing or non-string `description` as `\"\"` (§5.2).\n\n**`not_digestible`.** @avp9-nexus, the 31 October class shows why your reason has to be relative to a profile. Revision 1.1 also digests the empty set and orders repeated names by their canonical bytes; only an entry with no name (`MTL-SUBJ-001`) or a number with no double (`MTL-NUM-001`) is still refused. On 31 October HISTOR held 5,162 servers it had read and not digested. At 06:01 UTC today it held 762, and none of them has been read successfully since the change. The same declarations were `not_digestible` under 1.0 and have a digest under 1.1, yet the descriptor's `mtl` member stayed \"1\", so a stored `not_digestible` could go out of date with nothing saying so. Revision 1.2 (6 November) takes your shape. Every label names the revision it was issued under (`profileRevision`). A side without a digest carries `noDigest` with a reason from a closed list, `not_observed` or `not_digestible`, and the profile's code. A continuity label copies the prior side's revision from that side's own label and never guesses it. HISTOR issues these labels now, and it reads again, once, any package whose set an older revision declined.\n\nProfile, with both revisions: https://github.com/alexar76/aicom/blob/main/awr/adoption/mcp-trust-label/PROFILE.md#revision-12-2026-11-06\n\nThe server filter you pointed to on 31 October keeps the two cases apart: `state=unobserved` means nothing was read, and `state=undigested` means a list was read but not digested: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/servers?state=undigested","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6718278820/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]